															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hawkillo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5340588); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5340588&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606316074" alt="Hawkillo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5340588)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/hawkillo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5340588); return false;" >Hawkillo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606248734 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">24 Nov 2020 20:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4827221">
									

<p>This idea has been done quite a few times before, virus, end of the world, don't let it escape. What makes yours different? I advise you to check out <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-1462">SCP-1462</a> as it has a similar effect as yours. My critiques and final thoughts will be included in several collapsibles below.</p>
<hr />
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Critiques</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Critiques</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The container must be completely synthetic, and must be cleared of any organic matter, aside from SCP-XXX itself, any time that the container is opened.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You should remove the comma between synthetic &amp; and. You do not need a comma there because you have the word <em><strong>and</strong></em> there. Commas only need to be put before the word <em><strong>and</strong></em> if there is a list of three or more items or ideas. Instead format that sentence like this.</p>
<p><em>The container must be made of completely synthetic materials and must be cleared of any organic matter, aside from SCP-XXX itself, any time that the container is opened.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Lower clearances may enter, but only if accompanied by a level 3.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Level three what? Ogre?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>attempted termination of any subjects.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't think that the foundation would use an unknown disease to kill people. They would kill by firing squad. If they really wanted to use an scp they would just use 173 or something. Why risk a containment breach on something that can be done with a lot less work.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">To enter the wall, personnel must have level 2 clearance or higher.</span></p>
</blockquote>
<p>This should just be removed entirely. When a sentence is marked out there should be something after it that actually pertains to what the crossed out sentence was saying. Rather after yours you continue to explain stuff that has nothing to do with the prior sentence.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>access for personnel of level 3 clearance or above only, as to prevent any SCP-XXXX-A or SCP-XXXX-B instances from breaking out and wreaking havoc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would change the level clearance to level 4 if no experimentation or research is allowed as level three is just senior research personnel. Level four personnel are senior administrators tasked with keeping the foundation secure and preventing containment breaches.</p>
<p>Sorry for only critiquing your containment procedures but your article is rather long. Even though I couldn't give you tips on the rest I hope that this helps.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>-</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Final&nbsp;thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Final&nbsp;Thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I see this theme of virus SCPs pop up quite often. <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/system:page-tags/tag/virus">Here is a list of a few</a> and it can be very hard to take these common themes and make it interesting. I want you to read a few of these articles that have to do with viruses and take note of what you like and dislike about each. I also want to know what you will do for a plot line. In my opinion doing a virus/fungus SCP is so overdone it's going to be hard to make yours interesting and stand out from the rest. Good news is that there is an audience for everything. Definitely reach out to more experienced writers and critiquers for their thoughts as they will be a lot more helpful than I am and can help you set an interesting plot to go with the article. I hope you do well in your creative writing endeavors and have a good day!</p>
</div>
</div>
